## Primary boundary

Fresh [PBPS arXiv:2609.06905v1 Appendix C.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06905v1#A3.SS1),
HTML1197–1237, and the previously independently read Section2.2 equations2.6–2.14
and LemmaC.1 HTML1276–1310, distinguish actual reflected fibers, compact smooth
arguments and further H1/Poincare extensions. Fresh [SPHMC v1 Section4.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.SS1),
HTML403–430, leaves its actual RGO BL covariance producer separate. The proposed
cutoff distribution identities are an explicitly authored analytic prerequisite,
not a quoted numbered result or the source's only possible analysis route.

The reflected PBPS law has

\[
 W_y^{\rm ref}(z)=V((y+z)/2)+\|z-y\|^2/(8\eta),
\]

whereas the unreflected RGO law has
`W_y^{RGO}(x)=V(x)+norm(x-y)^2/(2eta)`. Under `z=2x-y`,
`dx=2^-d dz`, `Z_ref=2^d Z_RGO`, the reflected Hessian is
`(HessV((y+z)/2)+eta^-1 I)/4`, and gradient/Dirichlet pullback factors are2/4.
No equality of original-coordinate laws, domains, operators or epsilon
parameters follows without the true adapter. This localization step is
fiberwise and supplies no measurable selection in y.

## Seven-slot contract

| Slot | Exact bounded interface |
|---|---|
| Objects | Actual u, G=D.closure u and r=epsilon*u+inner(grad W,G)-f from the ordinary producer; actual cutoff chi and the three explicit localized functions below. |
| Domains | Finite real Hilbert/Borel full space incl0; input representatives are genuine weighted L2 and derived compact-volume L2; output classes and every test integral use global volume. |
| Quantifiers | D before epsilon/f, then one u/G/r before all cutoffs; each cutoff determines explicit v/Gchi/Fchi, not a replacement solution. Common R/S before y; D_y before epsilon/f in source consumer. |
| Assumptions | Smooth compact real chi; no positivity, mass normalization, supplied support/gradient/Laplacian bound, final localized PDE or H2 certificate. Generic localization consumes exact proved ordinary weak-gradient/PDE/localL2 interfaces. Shared potential remains C1; source V C2,0<alpha<=beta,eta>0,beta eta<=1. |
| Conclusions | True global-volume L2 of v/Gchi/Fchi, their derived compact support, all C1 compact directional weak-gradient identities and all C2 compact distribution PDE identities, each required L1 product proved. Preserve original solution/operator/kernel data. |
| Scope | Cutoff product/globalization producer only; no classical derivatives of u, weighted-closure membership of v, H2/core/Bochner/Poincare/BL, epsilon0, noncompact domains or main/composition credit. |
| Constants | Unit product-gradient coefficients and positive cross coefficient2 in Laplacian RHS. No uniform compact-bound constant or new curvature/time-scale requirement. |

For each real smooth compact cutoff chi, define the actual functions

\[
 v=\chi u,\qquad G_\chi=\chi G+u\nabla\chi,
 \qquad F_\chi=\chi r+2\langle\nabla\chi,G\rangle+u\Delta\chi.
\]

The target weak identities are

\[
 \int\psi\langle G_\chi,a\rangle dx
 =-\int vD\psi[a]dx\quad(\psi\in C_c^1,a\in E),
 \qquad\int v\Delta\phi dx=\int F_\chi\phi dx\quad(\phi\in C_c^2).
\]

Only chi/psi/phi have actual classical derivatives. Do not apply a total Lean
Laplacian to arbitrary u or assert that Gchi equals `D.closure(v)` merely
because it is an ordinary weak gradient. That additional same-weighted-domain
product statement would require its own closure-graph proof.

The correct source quantifier order is
`exists R,S; forall y; exists D_y; forall epsilon>0,f; exists u; forall chi`.
The solution depends on epsilon/f; it is not chosen before forcing. Moving chi
before the existential u loses the requested one-solution/all-cutoffs property.

## Smallest mathematical producer, six steps

1. Set K=`tsupport chi`. Prove grad chi and Delta chi continuous, bounded
   on K and zero off K by true derivative/support identities and finite-basis
   trace. All three localized functions vanish off K. Do not assume a compact
   Hessian/Laplacian certificate or silently replace weighted L2 by volume L2.
2. Use actual compact-restriction L2 of u/G/r plus bounded chi/grad chi/Delta
   chi and actual restricted a.e. measurability to prove v/Gchi/Fchi L2 on K.
   Then use measurable K and `memLp_indicator_iff_restrict`, proving each
   function equals its K-indicator, to obtain genuine global-volume MemLp.
   Actual `toLp` classes must retain the volume-a.e. representative identities.
3. Test the existing ordinary weak gradient with chi*psi. Prove the actual
   first derivative product rule and all individual L1 terms. Cancel the
   u*psi*Dchi[a] terms to derive the displayed Gchi identity. Global L2 alone
   is not global L1 on infinite volume; compact support/test bounds justify
   the products instead.
4. Prove the actual test product identity
   `Delta(chi*phi)=chi*Delta(phi)+2*inner(grad chi,grad phi)+phi*Delta(chi)`
   by genuine second derivatives and finite-basis trace. Test the original
   PDE with chi*phi, C2 compact. Integral splitting requires separate L1 of
   every term, not only integrability of their sum.
5. For each basis b_i use the ordinary weak gradient with
   `psi_i=phi*Dchi[b_i]`, a genuine C1 compact test. Summation yields
   `integral phi*inner(grad chi,G)=-integral u*inner(grad chi,grad phi)
   -integral u*phi*Delta(chi)`.
   Combining this with step4 produces exactly the positive coefficient2
   and the positive u*Delta(chi) term in Fchi. No differentiability of u is
   used. Both final PDE products must be genuinely volume L1.
6. Retain all original u/G/r, graph and source-kernel conclusions in the
   consumer. No convenience law, new D or cutoff-specific solution replaces
   the original witnesses. Their actual inherited local hypotheses are
   obtained from the source producer rather than imposed as new paper inputs.

C2 compact chi already suffices for this algebraic step; smooth compact chi
is appropriate for the later Schwartz/Fourier adapter. Neither sign nor
0<=chi<=1 nor integral chi=1 is mathematically required here. To recover local
regularity on a chosen region later, choose an unnormalized cutoff identically1
on an open neighborhood, not a mass-normalized mollifier or a cutoff merely
pointwise1 on an arbitrary boundary.

## Pinned API evidence and remaining adapters

Current public interfaces reread:
`OrdinaryWeakResolvent.weak_resolvent_laplacian` and
`ConditionalOrdinaryResolvent.conditional_ordinary_resolvent` provide actual
weak-gradient/PDE tests, local r squared-integrability and compact u/G/r L2.
Their source consumer produces true Gibbs/Gaussian J, common Markov R/S,
reflection, exact normalized W_y and original dense closable D_y.

Pinned Mathlib source contracts freshly inspected:

- `LpSeminorm/Indicator.lean:135`, `memLp_indicator_iff_restrict`:
  measurable-set indicator and restricted MemLp are genuinely equivalent.
  Combine with existing `MemLp.of_le_mul`/linear operations; support is still
  an actual proof obligation.
- `Calculus/FDeriv/Const.lean:378–392`: derivative support lies in the original
  topological support; derivative compact support is derived.
- `Calculus/ContDiff/FTaylorSeries.lean:948`: real second derivative evaluations;
  `InnerProductSpace/Laplacian.lean:172–197`: actual finite-basis trace.
  The bounded named search found no `laplacian_mul` in that Laplacian file.
  `laplacian_congr_nhds` at261 gives locality; no ready global product/PDE
  producer is assumed from the absence/presence of search matches.
- `Calculus/Gradient/Basic.lean:291,300`: Frechet/Riesz inner-gradient bridges;
  actual product calculus is still required for each smooth/test function.
- `BumpFunction/Basic.lean:137–170`: actual ordinary bump has compact support
  and is identically1 near every point of its inner open ball.
  `FiniteDimension.lean:464` has a finite-dimensional bump instance without
  a Nontrivial-space premise.

Zero dimension uses the empty basis, zero gradients/Laplacians, Gchi=0 and
Fchi=chi*r=0 a.e. by the inherited scalar PDE. Smooth functions are functions
on a singleton with compact support; normalization of the actual fiber remains
valid. No positive-dimension or unit-direction premise should be added to
reuse a radial cutoff helper. No zero-dimensional compile was run here.

## Why substantive; exact downstream boundary

This is more than restating a parent premise: it proves derivative-support and
restricted-to-global L2 adapters and nontrivial weak product identities with
correct coefficient2 for the same actual solution. A source consumer that
merely assumes these final localized identities or H2 would be a wrapper.
An abstract calculus leaf may consume the exact proved ordinary identities;
its real source consumer must obtain those inputs from the actual producer.

The immediate next Fourier/local-H2 dependency receives actual global-volume
L2 v/Fchi and compact support. Real-to-complex L2 classes, compact-to-Schwartz
extension using an outer cutoff1 near K, tempered-distribution embeddings and
exact Bessel/Fourier normalization remain separate proved adapters. The
Fourier dependency inventory records `B2=T-(2pi)^-2 Delta T`; neither that leaf
nor H2 is obtained by this note. Local H2 would still leave weighted global
Hessian control, same-operator core, global Bochner and centered epsilon limits
open. PBPS reflected and SPHMC unreflected BL consumers retain distinct law,
scale and operator contracts. No invariance/history/query-cost or either
main theorem/composition follows.
